        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Kosovo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">15 June 1389; Vidovdan, the day the battle is commemorated, falls on 28 June in the Gregorian calendar, and a Florentine letter of 20 October 1389 gives 28 June (St Vitus' day)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> field, about 5 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, in present-day Kosovo, on land ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>, leading estimate 27,000 to 30,000 men, with a higher estimate of 40,000; a coalition under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a>, leading estimate 15,000 to 20,000, with a higher estimate of 25,000. Both figures are disputed.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> were both killed and most of both armies were lost. Serbian manpower was depleted, and the Serbian principalities that were not already Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> became so in the following years.</span></div>
