    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>No account by a participant survives, and the earliest reports differ so much that the course of the battle cannot be reconstructed with confidence; estimates of the armies run from 15,000 to 20,000 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> and 27,000 to 30,000 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> was killed, the only time an Ottoman sultan died in battle, and tradition ascribes his death to the Serbian knight Miloš Obilić, though early accounts differ on who killed him and when.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> Myth, built on motifs of sacrifice, betrayal and heroism, grew from oral epic poetry and gained its modern importance in the 19th century, and Vidovdan is now an important Serbian national and religious holiday.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The battle is the only time in history an Ottoman sultan was killed in battle, yet a Florentine letter of 1389 names no killer, and an Italian work of 1416 says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

