    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1371</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> destroy much of the Serbian nobility at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a> dies childless in December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1386</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> are defeated at Pločnik, and again at Bileća in 1388</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1388</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> moves his troops from Philippoupolis to Ihtiman and on towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1389</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> arrives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a> on 14 June and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> brings his army to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> field</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1389</span><span class="rf-text">The battle is fought on 15 June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> are both killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1389</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia">Tvrtko I of Bosnia</a> writes to Trogir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> claiming a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1390</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> renew their campaign in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, 1390 to 1391</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1459</span><span class="rf-text">The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> on 20 June ends medieval Serbian statehood</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-kosovo" data-v="acf93f79d0" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up on Kosovo Field</b> Lazar held the Serbian centre, Branković the right and Vuković the left, with heavy cavalry in front. Murad stood behind his Janissaries in the centre, Bayezid held the Ottoman right, Yakub the left, and archers stood on both wings.</li><li><b>Ottoman archers shoot at the Serbian cavalry</b> The fighting began with Ottoman archers shooting at the Serbian cavalry, who then made ready to attack. The archers had been posted on the two wings of the Ottoman line.</li><li><b>The Serbian cavalry breaks the Ottoman left</b> After forming a wedge, the Serbian cavalry charged and broke through the Ottoman left wing under Yakub. It was less successful against the centre and the right wing.</li><li><b>Ottoman light troops counterattack</b> When the knights' charge was spent, light Ottoman cavalry and light infantry counterattacked, and the Serbian heavy armour became a disadvantage.</li><li><b>The Serbian centre pushes back the Ottomans</b> In the centre the Serbian troops pushed the Ottomans back. Bayezid's wing barely held off the Bosnians under Vuković, who inflicted heavy losses on the Ottomans.</li><li><b>Bayezid's counterattack pushes the Serbs back</b> The Ottomans, in a ferocious counterattack led by Bayezid, pushed the Serbian forces back. The Janissaries came forward again in the centre.</li><li><b>The Serbian infantry is routed</b> Later in the day the Ottomans prevailed and routed the Serbian infantry, which broke and fell back from the centre of the field.</li><li><b>The Bosnians drift towards the centre</b> Both flanks still held. Vuković's Bosnian troops drifted toward the centre to make up for the heavy losses of the Serbian infantry.</li><li><b>Branković withdraws</b> Branković saw that there was no hope of victory and withdrew from the field with as many of his men as he could save.</li><li><b>The remaining forces yield the field</b> Some time after Branković's retreat the remaining Bosnian and Serbian forces gave up the field, believing that victory was no longer possible.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan.jpg" alt="Stefan Dušan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Uro%C5%A1_IV_Du%C5%A1an_of_Serbia_Lesnovo_fresco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown 14th century Serbian artist, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Uroš IV Dušan</a>, who ruled from 1331 to 1355, was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>, whose reign saw the decline of central power and the rise of many virtually independent principalities. This period is known as the fall of the Serbian Empire. Uroš V could neither keep the empire his father had made nor repel foreign threats, and he died childless in December 1371, after much of the Serbian nobility had been destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> earlier that year.</p><p>Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a>, ruler of the northern part of the former empire, known as Moravian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, was aware of the Ottoman threat and began diplomatic and military preparations against the Ottomans. After Ottoman defeats at Pločnik in 1386 and Bileća in 1388, the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> moved his troops from Philippoupolis to Ihtiman in the spring of 1388, and on through Velbužd and Kratovo. The route was longer than the alternative through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> and the Nišava Valley, but it led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, one of the most important crossroads in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, from which Murad could attack the lands of either Lazar or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a>.</p><p>Murad marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a>, Preševo and Gjilan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, where he arrived on 14 June. Less is known of Lazar's preparations. He gathered his troops near Niš and, once he learned that Murad had moved to Velbužd, went across Prokuplje to Kosovo, a place that gave him control of all the routes Murad could take.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas.jpg" alt="Konstantin Dragaš" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas">Konstantin Dragaš</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SerbianMagnateKonstantinDragasDejanovic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RunnerTrees, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The composition of both armies is a matter of scholarly dispute, and no reliable information exists. The battle was nonetheless one of the largest of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It is generally believed that the Ottoman army was larger. The leading estimate accepted by Western scholars gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> 15,000 to 20,000 troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> 27,000 to 30,000; a higher estimate suggests 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and 25,000 for Lazar's coalition. The Ottoman historian Mehmed Neşri, writing in 1521, put the Christian coalition at around 500,000, double the Ottoman army; Noel Malcolm holds that Ottoman writers exaggerated it.</p><p>The Ottoman army was reinforced by Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, among them auxiliaries from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">Beylik</a> of Isfendiyar. It comprised no more than 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and as many as 8,500 cavalrymen, and it probably included the forces of two Serbian rulers who were Ottoman vassals, Marko Kraljević and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas">Konstantin Dejanović</a>. Murad led the centre, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> and the commander Evrenoz the European troops on the right wing, and his son Yakub the Anatolian troops on the left. About 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> fortified the wings, and the Janissaries held the centre.</p><p>Lazar's coalition was mainly his own Serbian contingent, troops of his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a>, and Bosnians under Vlatko Vuković sent by King Tvrtko I. Lazar led the centre, Branković the right and Vuković the left, which also held the foreign contingents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, Croatians, Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> are said to have joined, but Dejan Djokić later judged a broad coalition improbable.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kosovo&#8221; cites where it covers Army size, Ottoman army, Lazar's coalition, Troop deployment:</p><ul><li>N. Malcolm, Kosovo: A Short History (Macmillan, 1998)</li><li>John K. Cox, The History of Serbia (Greenwood, 2002)</li><li>Anna di Lellio, The Battle of Kosovo 1389: An Albanian Epic (I.B. Tauris, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Serbian and Turkish accounts differ, which makes the course of events hard to reconstruct. It is believed that the battle began with Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> shooting at the Serbian cavalry, who then prepared to attack. Formed in a wedge, the Serbian cavalry broke through the Ottoman left wing, which Yakub Çelebi commanded, but did less well against the centre and the right wing.</p><p>After the knights' charge was spent, light Ottoman cavalry and light infantry counterattacked, and the Serbian heavy armour became a disadvantage. In the centre the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> pushed back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a>'s wing, which barely held off the Bosnians under Vuković, who inflicted disproportionately heavy losses on it. Bayezid then led a fierce counterattack that pushed the Serbian forces back, and the Ottomans routed the Serbian infantry later in the day.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> is said to have left the field when he saw there was no hope of victory, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> had been captured, in order to save as many men as he could. Oral tradition calls this a betrayal, a view first set out by Mavro Orbini in 1601 and largely seen as unfounded. The remaining Bosnian and Serbian forces then yielded the field. In one of the earliest accounts twelve knights broke through the Ottoman defence, and one of them, later identified as Miloš Obilić, feigned desertion and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> with a hidden dagger before the sultan's bodyguards killed him. Another version has Obilić playing dead and stabbing Murad as he walked past. Sources also differ on whether this came early in the battle or after it had turned.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kosovo&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>John V. A. Fine, The Late Medieval Balkans: A Critical Survey from the Late Twelfth Century to the Ottoman Conquest (University of Michigan Press, 1994)</li><li>Clifford J. Rogers (ed.), Battle of Kosovo Polje (Oxford University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis.jpg" alt="Battle of Nicopolis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NikopolisSchlacht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sébastien Mamerot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Both armies were largely destroyed, and both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> lost their lives. Murad's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a>, hearing of his father's death, killed his younger brother Yakub Çelebi and became the sole heir to the throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> were left with too few men to defend their lands effectively, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had many more troops in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> tried to exploit the depletion of Serbian manpower by expanding in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and the Ottomans renewed their campaign in southern Serbia as early as 1390 to 1391.</p><p>The Serbian feudal class split in two. A northern faction favoured a conciliatory, pro-Ottoman policy as a defence against Hungary, and a southern faction, directly threatened by Ottoman expansion, looked to Hungary. Some principalities that were not already <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> became so, and their lords, among them Lazar's daughter, formed marriage ties with Bayezid. Lazar's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> became a loyal ally of Bayezid and contributed forces to his later campaigns, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Branković</a> had joined the anti-Ottoman coalition there, and Lazarević was rewarded with a large part of his lands. Branković died an Ottoman prisoner.</p><p>The news spread quickly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and early rumours dwelt on the death of the sultan. Tvrtko of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> wrote to the senate of Trogir on 1 August and to the council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> claiming a victory over the Ottomans. Over the following decades Serbia formally became an Ottoman province, and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> on 20 June 1459 marks the end of medieval Serbian statehood.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kosovo&#8221; cites where it covers Early reports, Geopolitical consequences:</p><ul><li>John V. A. Fine, The Late Medieval Balkans: A Critical Survey from the Late Twelfth Century to the Ottoman Conquest (University of Michigan Press, 1994)</li><li>Dejan Djokić, A Concise History of Serbia (Cambridge University Press, 2023)</li><li>Wayne S Vucinich, Kosovo: Legacy of a Medieval Battle (University of Minnesota, 1991)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No first-hand account by a participant exists. The contemporary sources come from widely diverging points of view, and they say little about tactics, army sizes or the field. European reports of the time were mostly concerned with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a> and a supposed Christian victory. The earliest Serbian sources owe more to religious rhetoric than to historical data. Every contemporary account was composed under religious, political and literary influences, and scholars can only try to trace the early strands of tradition that later became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> Myth.</p><p>The Florentine reply to Tvrtko of 20 October 1389 confirms that Murad was killed and that the battle took place on 28 June, St Vitus' day. It does not name the killer, but praises twelve loyal lords who cut their way to Murad's tent through a circle of chained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, and the one who stabbed the sultan. Mignanelli's work of 1416 says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> killed him. The first detailed Ottoman account is Neşri's, which exaggerates the size of Lazar's army and describes the battle as a significant Ottoman victory.</p><p>Who fought is disputed. Albanian historiography stresses the Albanian share, which on its reading of Ottoman sources may have been a quarter of Lazar's coalition, while Serbian historiography minimises or ignores it; the presence of Teodor II Muzaka is verified, that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-ii-balsic">Đurađ II Balšić</a> contested. Obilić may rest on a Hungarian knight, a claim modern scholars take seriously. Daniel Waley holds that next to nothing can be said with assurance about the numbers and multi-ethnic make-up of either army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria.jpg" alt="Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Schmutzer_-_Franz_Ferdinand_von_%C3%96sterreich-Este,_um_1914_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ferdinand Schmutzer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> Myth has long been central to Serbian folklore and literature, kept for centuries mainly in oral epic poetry and the songs of the guslars. It began soon after the event and grew from different originators into various versions. In the 19th century Vuk Karadžić collected the epic poems of the Kosovo cycle, which became the final version of the myth. Its modern telling rests on sacrifice (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a> choosing a heavenly kingdom over an earthly one), betrayal (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Branković</a>'s supposed desertion) and heroism (Obilić's killing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad</a>).</p><p>Miodrag Popović notes that in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in the 16th and 17th centuries the local population was Turkophilic, in a climate of adaptation to Ottoman rule, and did not read the legend as hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Serbian historiography came to simplify the political setting into a clash between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Perceptions changed with Serbian nationalism in the 19th century; for most of it the Principality of Serbia saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, not Kosovo, as its core. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 handed Bosnia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, leaving Kosovo as the only route for Serbian expansion, and this raised the Kosovo Myth to its modern status.</p><p>Vidovdan, celebrated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, is an important part of Serb national identity. Events on that day include Serbia's declaration of war on the Ottoman Empire in 1876, the assassination of Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> in 1914, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidovdan-constitution">Vidovdan Constitution</a> of 1921 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a>'s Gazimestan speech in 1989. Albanian epic songs in Kosovo also tell of the battle, centred on Obilić, called Millosh Kopiliqi.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Kosovo&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Sabrina P. Ramet, Civic and Uncivic Values: Serbia in the Post-Milosevic Era (Central European University Press, 2011)</li><li>Gerlachlus Duijzings, Religion and the Politics of Identity in Kosovo (C. Hurst &amp; Co., 2000)</li><li>Anna Di Lellio, The Battle of Kosovo 1389 (I.B. Tauris, 2009)</li><li>Albert Bates Lord, The Battle of Kosovo in Albanian and Serbocroatian Oral Epic Songs. (Columbia University Press, 1984)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> field in 1389 an Ottoman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> fought a coalition under the Serbian Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar</a>. Both leaders died and both armies were largely destroyed. Little reliable detail survives, but the battle has been central to Serbian memory, and Vidovdan commemorates it.</em></p>

